## Purpose and responsibility
Pantry list helps one household record named pantry supplies and decide what it needs to buy.
It owns a pantry list and a shopping list derived from the household's explicit needed choices.

## Household control
An item represents one named pantry supply.
Needed is the household's independent decision to buy an item, regardless of any stock it has.
The household controls whether an item is needed.
The shopping list contains the items marked as needing to buy.
Accept changes that make these choices clear and easy to review.
Resist changes that add quantities or stock levels to version one.

## Names people recognise
Accept names that let the household distinguish its supplies without a product catalogue.
Trim supplied names and compare their full names case-insensitively, reusing an existing matching item.
Variants remain distinct when their full supplied names differ, such as Brown rice and White rice.
Resist unnecessary duplicate entries that make the shopping list ambiguous.

## Local trust
Version one runs locally in a browser with no account, server or remote service.
The first useful journey is to add Rice, mark it needed, view it on the shopping list, then reload and retain it.
Accept changes that preserve the household's saved choices across reloads.
When saving fails, keep the current session usable and expose unsaved changes.
Claim reload retention only after saving succeeds.

## Boundaries and change review
Accounts, remote sharing, quantities and stock tracking are outside version one.
Accept a change when it helps the household record supplies or identify what to buy while preserving clear choices and honest saving status.
Resist a change when it expands those responsibilities or makes the shopping list depend on information the household has not chosen to maintain.

