# Pantry list vision

## Purpose and responsibility
Pantry list helps one household record named pantry supplies and decide what it needs to buy.
It owns a pantry list and a shopping list derived from the household's explicit needed choices.

## Household control
An item represents one named pantry supply.
Needed is the household's independent decision to buy an item, regardless of any stock it has.
The household controls whether an item is needed.
The shopping list contains the items marked as needing to buy.
Accept changes that make these choices clear and easy to review.
Resist changes that add quantities or stock levels to version one.

## Names people recognise
Accept names that let the household distinguish its supplies without a product catalogue.
Trim supplied names and compare their full names case-insensitively, reusing an existing matching item.
Variants remain distinct when their full supplied names differ, such as Brown rice and White rice.
Resist unnecessary duplicate entries that make the shopping list ambiguous.

## Local trust
Version one runs locally in a browser with no account, server or remote service.
The first useful journey is to add Rice, mark it needed, view it on the shopping list, then reload and retain it.
Accept changes that preserve the household's saved choices across reloads.
When saving fails, keep the current session usable and expose unsaved changes.
Claim reload retention only after saving succeeds.
If saved household data cannot be read, preserve it until the household explicitly chooses a reset.
Expose the problem and keep current session changes usable and visibly unsaved without overwriting the unreadable saved data.

## Boundaries and change review
Accounts, remote sharing, quantities and stock tracking are outside version one.
Accept a change when it helps the household record supplies or identify what to buy while preserving clear choices and honest saving status.
Resist a change when it expands those responsibilities or makes the shopping list depend on information the household has not chosen to maintain.
# Pantry language

**Item**: One named pantry supply.

**Needed**: The household has marked an item as needing to buy, independently of stock.

**Shopping list**: The items marked as needing to buy.
# Pantry design

## Responsibilities

The UI renders pantry choices and submits actions through the public pantry API.
`src/pantry.ts` owns name identity, needed choices, session state and saving status.
`src/storage/private.ts` owns the local saved representation and validates it before exposing items.
`src/main.ts` wires the pantry owner to the browser UI.
Data flows from a named input to pantry actions, to saving through the private adapter, and back through snapshots to pantry and shopping lists.
Storage has one writer; the UI never reads or writes it.

## UI boundary

UI modules import only the public `../pantry.js` API.
UI modules cannot import private storage or access localStorage directly.
Browser rendering APIs, including document creation, DOM events and confirmation dialogs, are permitted.
ESLint configuration is the executable authority for this boundary; no blanket browser-global ban is used.
No path aliases or dynamic UI imports are supported; adding one requires a reviewed rule and proof update.
Computed runtime access through arbitrary aliases or reflection is outside static lint coverage and remains forbidden in review.
There are no exceptions to the UI boundary in version one.

## Acceptance

Rice journey: add Rice, mark it needed, view Rice on the shopping list, reload and retain it after successful saving.
Names: reuse trimmed case-insensitive full-name matches; Brown rice and White rice remain distinct.
Needed: an explicit buying choice independently controls shopping membership, with no quantities or stock levels.
Save failure: keep session actions usable, expose unsaved changes, offer retry, and claim retention only after saving succeeds.
Unreadable saved data: preserve original saved bytes through edits and cancelled reset; replace only after explicit household confirmation, then save current session choices.
`tests/journey.test.mjs` drives these outcomes on the real browser UI and retains screenshots and observations in unique external scratch.
A change is complete when affected acceptance cases pass on the final inputs; passing unit checks alone cannot establish the household journey.

## Review and maintenance

VISION.md governs scope; GLOSSARY.md governs meanings; this design governs responsibilities and acceptance.
Accept additional dependencies or abstractions only for a current approved responsibility with a simpler alternative considered; no exception without a documented reason, narrow scope and removal condition.
The selected first maintenance contract is `.bstack/project.json`; it maps every owned path to the sources and the native aggregate `npm run check`.
Formatting belongs to Prettier, syntax and the UI boundary to ESLint, and types and build to TypeScript.
`node .bstack/bin/bstack-check.mjs --repo . --base <base> --assessment .bstack/assessment.json --json` runs portable reviewed maintenance evidence and fresh required checks with no skill installation.
The assessment records substantive document impacts and explicit unmapped paths; semantic review is local author review, with no hosted CI or delivery service.
Initial contract/checker adoption is the selected foundation migration; there is no previous contract or checker to run, and native acceptance coverage must be retained explicitly.
After initial creation, commit implementation, collect a portable assessment against the explicit foundation comparison base, review and bind its fingerprint, and commit assessment separately.

## Environment and limits

The product has no backend, account or remote dependency; local storage belongs to one browser origin.
The local preview serves static files over loopback; it is development infrastructure and owns no pantry state.
Node 24+, npm and a Chromium browser are development prerequisites; set CHROME_PATH when Chromium is not `/usr/bin/google-chrome`.
Persistence across different browsers, profiles, origins or devices and concurrent tabs are outside the first journey.
Native suite guard requires Linux resource observations: at least 8 GiB available memory, 20 GiB free disk, load below 36 and at most two host-wide Node suites including the proposed run.
The guard prints resource totals and suite counts, never host process inventories.
Native checks are pinned development dependencies; the standalone checker itself depends only on Node and Git.
# Pantry list

A local household pantry list and shopping list, without quantities or stock levels.

## Setup and use

Use Node 24+, npm, Git and Chromium (default `/usr/bin/google-chrome`; override with CHROME_PATH).
Run `npm ci --cache .npm-cache --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund`, then `npm run build` and `npm start`.
Open http://127.0.0.1:4173, add Rice, mark Rice needed, check the shopping list and reload.
Keep the browser session open while changes are unsaved; retry saving or explicitly replace unreadable saved data only when you choose to do so.

## Verification

Run `npm run check` for formatting, lint, types, build and observed browser journeys.
Run `npm run journey` for the browser cases after building.
Capture locations are printed by the journey suite; screenshots and observations stay in unique external scratch.
Checks enforce the resource prerequisites before Node suites run.
See [vision](VISION.md), [glossary](GLOSSARY.md) and [design](DESIGN.md) for authority, acceptance and maintenance.
The maintained dependency-free checker is `.bstack/bin/bstack-check.mjs`; see DESIGN.md for its explicit base and assessment command.
